e. The victory margin can imply very different political environments irrespective of the eventual winner. The runoff margin can therefore serve as a compact indicator of electoral polarization, the breadth of the eventual winner’s coalition, and the degree to which the electorate remains evenly divided at the end of the campaign.\n\nA [Datafolha poll](https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/lula-maintains-lead-over-flavio-bolsonaro-ahead-brazil-election-datafolha-poll-2026-09-17/) on September 17 reported Lula at 46% and Bolsonaro at 44% in a hypothetical second-round matchup, while [other recent polls](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opinion_polling_for_the_2026_Brazilian_presidential_election) have also shown narrow margins and variation in which candidate holds a numerical lead. The runoff margin can move materially during the remaining campaign because of candidate debates, endorsements, economic news, campaign strategy, first-round vote transfers, and changes in turnout among different voter blocs.","resolution_criteria":"This question will resolve as the difference, in percentage points of valid votes, between the first- and second-place candidates in the Brazlian national presidential election held on October 25, 2026, published by Brazil’s [Tribunal Superior Eleitoral (TSE)](https://www.tse.jus.br/eleicoes/informacoes-tecnicas-sobre-a-divulgacao-de-resultados).\n\nThe value will be calculated as: winner’s valid-vote share − runner-up’s valid-vote share. For example, if the winner receives 52.4% of valid votes and the runner-up receives 47.6%, the question resolves to 4.8 percentage points.","fine_print":"[The TSE](https://www.tse.jus.br/comunicacao/noticias/2026/Marco/eleicoes-2026-confira-as-principais-datas-do-calendario-eleitoral) defines valid votes as votes cast for candidates, excluding blank and null ballots. The final nationwide TSE presidential result will be used including any corrected official figures.","short_title":"What will be the winning margi